00:04:47.750 --> 00:04:54.630
And just as a reminder, you want to update w and b simultaneously on each step.

00:04:54.630 --> 00:04:58.510
Now let's get familiar with how gradient descent works.

00:04:58.510 --> 00:05:03.590
One issue we saw with gradient descent is that it can lead to a local minimum instead

00:05:03.590 --> 00:05:08.990
of a global minimum, where the global minimum means the point that has the lowest possible

00:05:08.990 --> 00:05:13.390
value for the cost function j out of all possible points.

00:05:13.390 --> 00:05:18.630
You may recall this surface plot that looks like an outdoor park with a few hills with

00:05:18.630 --> 00:05:22.030
the grass and the birds is a relaxing outdoor hill.

00:05:22.030 --> 00:05:25.430
This function has more than one local minimum.

00:05:25.430 --> 00:05:30.630
Remember depending on where you initialize the parameters w and b, you can end up at

00:05:30.630 --> 00:05:32.510
different local minimum.

00:05:32.510 --> 00:05:36.950
You can end up here, or you can end up here.

00:05:36.950 --> 00:05:42.230
But it turns out when you're using a squared error cost function with linear regression,

00:05:42.230 --> 00:05:47.550
the cost function does not and will never have multiple local minimum.

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:52.430
It has a single global minimum because of this bow shape.

00:05:52.430 --> 00:05:58.870
The technical term for this is that this cost function is a convex function.

00:05:58.870 --> 00:06:05.630
Informally, a convex function is a bow shaped function and it cannot have any local minimum

00:06:05.630 --> 00:06:09.390
other than the single global minimum.

00:06:09.390 --> 00:06:16.030
So when you implement gradient descent on a convex function, one nice property is that

00:06:16.030 --> 00:06:22.510
so long as your learning rate is chosen appropriately, it will always converge to the global minimum.

